WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:04.540
It's great to welcome back to the program
today, Dr. Isaac Veau Ghosh, who's an infectious

00:00:04.540 --> 00:00:09.980
disease physician and scientist based at the
University of Toronto and Toronto General

00:00:09.980 --> 00:00:11.230
Hospital.

00:00:11.230 --> 00:00:12.230
Great to have you back on.

00:00:12.230 --> 00:00:13.550
Thanks for having me back.

00:00:13.550 --> 00:00:17.840
So we spoke recently, but everything has changed
since we spoke.

00:00:17.840 --> 00:00:24.249
I mean, the degree to which this has has continued
to spread as someone who works in this field.

00:00:24.249 --> 00:00:28.029
Are you surprised by the trajectory since
we've spoken?

00:00:28.029 --> 00:00:30.090
Sadly, no.

00:00:30.090 --> 00:00:32.500
We knew that this was going to spread around
the world.

00:00:32.500 --> 00:00:36.230
We knew that countries that were reporting
cases were going to have a growing burden

00:00:36.230 --> 00:00:37.280
of cases.

00:00:37.280 --> 00:00:40.789
And then we knew that countries that didn't
yet have cases, we're going to start to see

00:00:40.789 --> 00:00:42.890
they were going to start to see cases very
shortly.

00:00:42.890 --> 00:00:46.199
And, you know, we take a step back and look
at the 30000 foot view.

00:00:46.199 --> 00:00:51.879
Now, you know, most of the world is affected
and sadly, some places are more severely affected

00:00:51.879 --> 00:00:52.910
than others.

00:00:52.910 --> 00:00:53.910
So let's start there.

00:00:53.910 --> 00:00:55.910
I have a bunch of questions, including from
my audience.

00:00:55.910 --> 00:01:00.920
I'm not going through them in order of importance,
but just to start somewhere, we're seeing

00:01:00.920 --> 00:01:04.659
very different death rates in different countries.

00:01:04.659 --> 00:01:09.670
And a lot of ideas have been posited that
there's two different strains of different

00:01:09.670 --> 00:01:16.880
lethality, although I've not seen good evidence
for that so far that the populations are older

00:01:16.880 --> 00:01:19.170
or sicker in some places.

00:01:19.170 --> 00:01:23.439
What do we know so far about the drastically
differing death rates?

00:01:23.439 --> 00:01:24.439
Yeah.

00:01:24.439 --> 00:01:29.400
So short answer is that I you know, we don't
know as a medical and scientific community,

00:01:29.400 --> 00:01:30.850
but we have lots of different theories.

00:01:30.850 --> 00:01:36.070
And I think this will be fascinating when
the dust settles and, you know, a year or

00:01:36.070 --> 00:01:37.790
two from now we have better answers.

00:01:37.790 --> 00:01:40.780
So I still think we have to remain humble
that we don't have all the answers.

00:01:40.780 --> 00:01:44.670
Having said that, I think there's several
factors that come into play here.

00:01:44.670 --> 00:01:48.909
This business of having several strains of
the virus, I'm not entirely sure that's going

00:01:48.909 --> 00:01:49.909
to play out.

00:01:49.909 --> 00:01:52.289
I don't think that's going to pan out in the
end.

00:01:52.289 --> 00:01:53.289
Viruses mutate all the time.

00:01:53.289 --> 00:01:57.740
It doesn't necessarily mean that it's more
virulent or more deadly than another virus.

00:01:57.740 --> 00:02:02.149
And there's likely multiple strains of this
virus as it spreads through different continents.

00:02:02.149 --> 00:02:07.049
It doesn't necessarily mean that those viruses
will cause different severity of illness.

00:02:07.049 --> 00:02:08.239
So that's one point.

00:02:08.239 --> 00:02:14.320
The second point is we know that the populations
that this virus is affecting are different.

00:02:14.320 --> 00:02:19.150
And we definitely know that this virus has
more severe outcomes in people who are over

00:02:19.150 --> 00:02:22.480
the age of 60 and especially in those over
the age of 80.

00:02:22.480 --> 00:02:28.950
So elderly populations, unfortunately, are
getting significantly impacted by this infection.

00:02:28.950 --> 00:02:35.040
And we've seen, you know, for example, places
like Italy where there is tremendous sadly,

00:02:35.040 --> 00:02:40.790
this tremendous death toll and this is vastly
overrepresented in elderly people.

00:02:40.790 --> 00:02:45.239
And I think the third major factor here is
how robust the health care system is and how

00:02:45.239 --> 00:02:49.330
robust a public health system is within a
country.

00:02:49.330 --> 00:02:57.239
And we've seen countries, for example, like
South Korea, Singapore, Hong Kong, Taiwan,

00:02:57.239 --> 00:03:04.409
where they have fantastic clinical abilities
and clinical capabilities and also a very

00:03:04.409 --> 00:03:06.200
robust public health system.

00:03:06.200 --> 00:03:11.730
And they really implemented strong control
initiatives earlier on in the epidemic.

00:03:11.730 --> 00:03:16.500
And and most of those countries, I mean, everyone's
coming out battered and bruised, but they're

00:03:16.500 --> 00:03:20.150
coming out less battered and bruised compared
to most others.

00:03:20.150 --> 00:03:24.950
So to follow up on on some of the discrepancies
that we're seeing before we move on to testing

00:03:24.950 --> 00:03:32.019
early, the data from China suggested a significantly
higher death rate for men than women.

00:03:32.019 --> 00:03:37.659
And it was suggested that for cultural reasons,
men are more likely to smoke in China and

00:03:37.659 --> 00:03:40.080
that that might be the explanation.

00:03:40.080 --> 00:03:46.099
But it looks like that discrepancy is persisting
in other countries without the cultural smoking

00:03:46.099 --> 00:03:47.099
differences.

00:03:47.099 --> 00:03:49.190
What what do we know about that so far?

00:03:49.190 --> 00:03:52.989
You know, it's unclear yet, and I don't have
a good answer for that yet.

00:03:52.989 --> 00:03:59.090
And that is obviously one of the top questions
is what are the actual risk factors for having

00:03:59.090 --> 00:04:01.519
a serious infection and death?

00:04:01.519 --> 00:04:07.159
We already know that age is a risk factor
and that medical co-morbidities are a risk

00:04:07.159 --> 00:04:08.159
factor.

00:04:08.159 --> 00:04:11.720
You know, if you're if you're sick to begin
with and you get this infection, you're more

00:04:11.720 --> 00:04:16.110
likely to have a more serious infection and
sadly more likely to succumb to this infection.

00:04:16.110 --> 00:04:25.230
But sex and gender are still unclear as to
how that really manifests with this infection.

00:04:25.230 --> 00:04:27.530
And by virtue of being a male.

00:04:27.530 --> 00:04:34.730
Is that also reflective of having a greater,
you know, depending on the country, a greater

00:04:34.730 --> 00:04:38.110
probability of having hypertension, diabetes,
smoking?

00:04:38.110 --> 00:04:42.210
Or is there just something about being male
that puts puts one at greater risk of having

00:04:42.210 --> 00:04:43.350
a more severe outcome?

00:04:43.350 --> 00:04:45.130
It's not it's not yet clear.

00:04:45.130 --> 00:04:47.670
So let's talk a little bit about testing.

00:04:47.670 --> 00:04:52.300
We know that here in the United States, we
have been drastically under testing.

00:04:52.300 --> 00:04:54.660
So no surprise as more tests.

00:04:54.660 --> 00:04:59.490
Rolls out the number of cases start to increase
dramatically, but probably still drastically

00:04:59.490 --> 00:05:05.560
under representing the real number of cases,
we are hearing from some governments that

00:05:05.560 --> 00:05:09.350
there's really no reason to test people without
symptoms.

00:05:09.350 --> 00:05:13.530
There's no reason to test people who are not
serious enough to be in the hospital.

00:05:13.530 --> 00:05:16.110
I want to hear your thought about that in
general.

00:05:16.110 --> 00:05:18.750
But then also more specifically.

00:05:18.750 --> 00:05:24.240
Most of the tests in the US are not antibody
tests, which wouldn't tell us if someone has

00:05:24.240 --> 00:05:27.730
had and has recovered from the disease.

00:05:27.730 --> 00:05:34.560
Would the issue of testing everybody be different
if we were using an antibody test rather than

00:05:34.560 --> 00:05:36.050
a pathogen test?

00:05:36.050 --> 00:05:42.050
OK, so there's several excellent components
of that question that I think need to be addressed.

00:05:42.050 --> 00:05:47.110
The first component is the degree of testing,
and I agree with you completely.

00:05:47.110 --> 00:05:51.530
There is tremendous under testing, not only
in the United States, but actually in most

00:05:51.530 --> 00:05:52.780
parts of the world.

00:05:52.780 --> 00:05:58.670
So the actual figures that we see, the numbers
that are reported are gross underestimates

00:05:58.670 --> 00:06:02.990
of the true burden of infection in just about
anywhere in the world.

00:06:02.990 --> 00:06:06.440
Now, some places have more access to diagnostic
tests than others.

00:06:06.440 --> 00:06:12.790
For example, if we compare the United States
with South Korea, the United States is testing

00:06:12.790 --> 00:06:18.550
relatively fewer people, significantly fewer
people compared to a place like South Korea.

00:06:18.550 --> 00:06:24.890
So, you know, we still so South Korea's numbers
are probably more of an accurate reflection

00:06:24.890 --> 00:06:30.300
of the true burden of this disease in that
country relative to the numbers coming out

00:06:30.300 --> 00:06:31.340
of the United States.

00:06:31.340 --> 00:06:32.340
But we get it.

00:06:32.340 --> 00:06:33.340
We all know that.

00:06:33.340 --> 00:06:40.670
And, you know, there's various reasons for
why some countries have imposed broader diagnostic

00:06:40.670 --> 00:06:42.590
testing than others in the United States.

00:06:42.590 --> 00:06:48.500
Again, these are completely a political statement,
but there was just some incredible bureaucratic,

00:06:48.500 --> 00:06:54.300
logistic and economic hurdles that got in
the way and that continue to get in the way

00:06:54.300 --> 00:06:57.470
of providing wide diagnostic testing.

00:06:57.470 --> 00:07:01.970
And that certainly has set the United States
back in their fight against the Cauvin 19

00:07:01.970 --> 00:07:02.970
pandemic.

00:07:02.970 --> 00:07:07.200
And it's really unfortunate to watch that
unfold in real time.

00:07:07.200 --> 00:07:09.300
But by no means are other countries perfect.

00:07:09.300 --> 00:07:15.760
There's many, many reasons why places just
just have not tested broadly.

00:07:15.760 --> 00:07:20.360
The next point I think that's important to
address is the utility of the test that we're

00:07:20.360 --> 00:07:21.360
doing.

00:07:21.360 --> 00:07:26.670
And I think we have to be mindful of the question
we're asking in a clinical sense.

00:07:26.670 --> 00:07:30.660
One of the questions we ask is, do you have
this infection?

00:07:30.660 --> 00:07:31.760
Yes or no?

00:07:31.760 --> 00:07:36.650
Because that is going to impact our clinical
manage and management of that individual now

00:07:36.650 --> 00:07:39.230
and also in the coming days.

00:07:39.230 --> 00:07:44.640
So the testing that's being done now, which
is which is a PCR test, which basically answers

00:07:44.640 --> 00:07:47.540
that question, is that virus present in you?

00:07:47.540 --> 00:07:48.540
Yes or no?

00:07:48.540 --> 00:07:49.540
Are you infected?

00:07:49.540 --> 00:07:50.540
Yes or no?

00:07:50.540 --> 00:07:51.540
That's an extremely important test.

00:07:51.540 --> 00:07:53.830
And that's the appropriate test to be doing
right now.

00:07:53.830 --> 00:07:57.130
And it's you know, it's not the most comfortable
test to do what's called a nasal pharyngeal

00:07:57.130 --> 00:08:02.710
swab, where they take a big swab and they
stick it all the way into the back of your

00:08:02.710 --> 00:08:03.710
nose.

00:08:03.710 --> 00:08:05.450
People think it just like a little thing in
the front of their nose.

00:08:05.450 --> 00:08:06.450
No.

00:08:06.450 --> 00:08:08.510
Anyone that's had this knows that it's not
the most comfortable test to have, but you've

00:08:08.510 --> 00:08:10.180
got to get a good sample.

00:08:10.180 --> 00:08:11.350
And that's the way to do it.

00:08:11.350 --> 00:08:15.430
And that's the appropriate has to be doing,
because that answered the question, yes or

00:08:15.430 --> 00:08:16.430
no?

00:08:16.430 --> 00:08:18.550
Am I infected with this virus?

00:08:18.550 --> 00:08:24.540
And that will impact clinical management of
that individual now and also in the foreseeable

00:08:24.540 --> 00:08:26.600
days, days ahead.

00:08:26.600 --> 00:08:30.650
What you're referring to as well is what's
called an antibody test, which basically asks

00:08:30.650 --> 00:08:36.400
the question, have you ever been infected
with this virus?

00:08:36.400 --> 00:08:37.400
Yes or no?

00:08:37.400 --> 00:08:38.400
It doesn't tell you.

00:08:38.400 --> 00:08:40.740
Are you currently infected with this virus?

00:08:40.740 --> 00:08:41.740
It's.

00:08:41.740 --> 00:08:43.760
Have I ever been infected with this virus?

00:08:43.760 --> 00:08:45.070
Yes or no?

00:08:45.070 --> 00:08:48.530
Antibody tests or typically blood tests.

00:08:48.530 --> 00:08:54.870
And there are many groups that are developing
these antibody tests to determine if someone

00:08:54.870 --> 00:08:58.990
has ever had an exposure to this particular
corona virus.

00:08:58.990 --> 00:08:59.990
Yes or no?

00:08:59.990 --> 00:09:03.940
These tests are hard to develop because as
you can imagine, there's lots of different

00:09:03.940 --> 00:09:05.760
types of corona viruses.

00:09:05.760 --> 00:09:10.210
We have the common cold, which has there's
four different types of corona viruses that

00:09:10.210 --> 00:09:11.830
are implicated in the common cold.

00:09:11.830 --> 00:09:15.430
Plus, we have this covered 19 pandemic.

00:09:15.430 --> 00:09:20.900
And, you know, we've heard about birds and
stars and other corona viruses as well.

00:09:20.900 --> 00:09:26.240
So one of the issues is, is there cross reactivity
when we're looking for this specific corona

00:09:26.240 --> 00:09:30.480
virus versus other common corona viruses that
cause the common cold?

00:09:30.480 --> 00:09:35.340
You know, I think there's a lot of research
teams that are developing these serologic

00:09:35.340 --> 00:09:36.340
tests.

00:09:36.340 --> 00:09:37.580
These are excellent tests to have.

00:09:37.580 --> 00:09:43.140
I think at the end of the day, we'll be very,
very valuable to use those tests widely to

00:09:43.140 --> 00:09:50.690
see truly what proportion of the population
was affected by this COBA 19 pandemic.

00:09:50.690 --> 00:09:54.580
But, you know, in terms of clinical management.

00:09:54.580 --> 00:09:59.160
Right now, I don't think the serology tests
would be nearly as useful as the PCR test

00:09:59.160 --> 00:10:00.160
that we're doing right now.

00:10:00.160 --> 00:10:04.080
Yeah, I mean, I guess we're we're my we're
I'm thinking about it would be.

00:10:04.080 --> 00:10:08.931
Imagine that you tested large portions of
the population and found that, you know, imagine

00:10:08.931 --> 00:10:13.550
it's one and a half or two percent of the
population had the antibodies.

00:10:13.550 --> 00:10:17.980
Would that be useful from an infectious disease
perspective?

00:10:17.980 --> 00:10:22.230
In thinking about the trajectory, so to speak?

00:10:22.230 --> 00:10:23.380
Yes, absolutely.

00:10:23.380 --> 00:10:30.200
So I just want to distinguish two sort of
overlapping Venn diagrams of clinical care

00:10:30.200 --> 00:10:31.430
and public health.

00:10:31.430 --> 00:10:33.980
And of course, these overlaps significantly.

00:10:33.980 --> 00:10:39.800
But from a clinical standpoint, that antibody
test may be a little bit helpful, but the

00:10:39.800 --> 00:10:42.800
PCR test would be extraordinarily helpful.

00:10:42.800 --> 00:10:45.970
It's also available, you know, in decent labs.

00:10:45.970 --> 00:10:49.560
It can be available in a matter of hours.

00:10:49.560 --> 00:10:52.660
So the PCR test is valuable from that standpoint.

00:10:52.660 --> 00:10:56.840
But when we're looking at, you know, populations
and, you know, as you point out, if we knew

00:10:56.840 --> 00:11:02.500
that one population had much greater exposure
to this virus compared to other populations.

00:11:02.500 --> 00:11:06.150
Yeah, it's also extremely valuable from a
public health standpoint because that can

00:11:06.150 --> 00:11:12.380
help drive policy, that could help guide public
health policy, that could drive, you know,

00:11:12.380 --> 00:11:14.900
vaccine efforts when a vaccine is available.

00:11:14.900 --> 00:11:20.580
That could also drive more local public health
initiatives such as the physical or social

00:11:20.580 --> 00:11:24.870
distancing measures that we're all talking
about and employing widely these days.

00:11:24.870 --> 00:11:29.570
So, you know, it's certainly valuable information,
but I think from a public health standpoint,

00:11:29.570 --> 00:11:32.620
the serology test would be more valuable at
this point in time.

00:11:32.620 --> 00:11:35.760
And from a clinical standpoint, the PCR is
a little bit more valuable.

00:11:35.760 --> 00:11:41.520
We're gonna go to a break on our TV and radio
show and podcast with Dr. Isaac Ghosh put

00:11:41.520 --> 00:11:47.720
we'll continue talking on our YouTube channel
where you can find the remainder of our conversation.

00:11:47.720 --> 00:11:53.950
So then we get into the social distancing
methods and the questions that are that are

00:11:53.950 --> 00:11:59.220
now coming up about should you go get groceries
or have them delivered?

00:11:59.220 --> 00:12:03.870
And how do you weigh or manage the exposure
of being in a grocery store versus someone

00:12:03.870 --> 00:12:07.740
you don't know touching your fruits and vegetables
in this type of thing?

00:12:07.740 --> 00:12:16.080
At what degree at what level of granularity
do these decisions make an impact, so to speak?

00:12:16.080 --> 00:12:20.810
Yeah, I mean, obviously, these are very challenging
questions because it's hard to micromanage

00:12:20.810 --> 00:12:23.860
every potential situation that may arise.

00:12:23.860 --> 00:12:27.720
But I think if people are mindful of the general
principles, we'll all be OK.

00:12:27.720 --> 00:12:31.380
And the general principles are, you know,
physical distance.

00:12:31.380 --> 00:12:34.470
I know we're talking about social distancing.

00:12:34.470 --> 00:12:36.780
But I think it's important words do matter.

00:12:36.780 --> 00:12:41.570
And I think we're switching to using a physical
distancing measure because social interaction

00:12:41.570 --> 00:12:46.730
is extraordinarily important and especially
important during these very difficult times

00:12:46.730 --> 00:12:49.410
where we are physically separated from one
another.

00:12:49.410 --> 00:12:53.720
So when we're talking about these, you know,
physical distancing measures, I think as the

00:12:53.720 --> 00:12:58.390
public health officials have been saying,
you know, keep it to about six feet if possible.

00:12:58.390 --> 00:13:02.950
Of course, we can't micro regulate every potential
interaction that may happen.

00:13:02.950 --> 00:13:08.200
Of course, people need to go out and get groceries
and, you know, fill their car with gas and,

00:13:08.200 --> 00:13:13.430
you know, still live their lives with, you
know, as much normalcy as possible.

00:13:13.430 --> 00:13:18.930
But, of course, we also have to abide by these
rules to ensure personal safety and also for

00:13:18.930 --> 00:13:19.930
public safety.

00:13:19.930 --> 00:13:25.300
So, you know, grocery stores, many grocery
stores have adapted to this as well.

00:13:25.300 --> 00:13:28.830
And you can tell that many people are going
to grocery stores at all hours.

00:13:28.830 --> 00:13:32.850
Some grocery stores I've seen have taken very
creative solution in the lineup.

00:13:32.850 --> 00:13:37.290
They put tape on the floor so that they know
that people will stand in line and be separated

00:13:37.290 --> 00:13:39.470
by an appropriate amount of space.

00:13:39.470 --> 00:13:43.250
Many grocery stores have actually put some
hand sanitizer out.

00:13:43.250 --> 00:13:45.310
And there's also the onus on the individual.

00:13:45.310 --> 00:13:49.300
If you're going to go to a grocery store is
going to a public place to get essential items

00:13:49.300 --> 00:13:54.540
that you may need, bring some hand sanitizer
if you're in touch with any high contact surfaces.

00:13:54.540 --> 00:13:55.600
Let's be mindful of that.

00:13:55.600 --> 00:13:58.460
Wash your hands with soap and water or use
hand sanitizer.

00:13:58.460 --> 00:14:00.430
Be mindful about not touching your face.

00:14:00.430 --> 00:14:06.330
And, of course, wash down any surfaces in
your home as well with, you know, the appropriate

00:14:06.330 --> 00:14:08.980
bleach based solutions that that are that
are recommended.

00:14:08.980 --> 00:14:12.810
So there's lots of things that we can do that
we can do that essentially mitigate our risk

00:14:12.810 --> 00:14:18.360
of getting this infection while we practice
our physical distancing measures and simultaneously

00:14:18.360 --> 00:14:22.960
while we go about our day to day lives and
try to care for ourselves and our families.

00:14:22.960 --> 00:14:29.490
So last time you were on, we talked about
the question of droplet versus aerosol is

00:14:29.490 --> 00:14:31.661
is a gradient, so to speak.

00:14:31.661 --> 00:14:38.500
It's not just it's one or the other as that
applies to, for example, people in Boston

00:14:38.500 --> 00:14:40.390
as the weather has been nicer.

00:14:40.390 --> 00:14:41.930
Ah, ah, ah.

00:14:41.930 --> 00:14:45.660
Distancing physically, but are going to parks
and bike paths, for example.

00:14:45.660 --> 00:14:50.340
And for the most part, people know you kind
of stay separate as you pass each other.

00:14:50.340 --> 00:14:54.180
But invariably when the bike path is three
feet wide, runners are going to pass each

00:14:54.180 --> 00:14:58.450
other in the in the opposite direction and
they're going to be breathing as people tend

00:14:58.450 --> 00:15:00.980
to do when when they are running.

00:15:00.980 --> 00:15:04.740
What is the risk of an infection in these
outdoor scenarios?

00:15:04.740 --> 00:15:08.360
I've read it's as low as 1 percent, but it's
like, what does that really mean?

00:15:08.360 --> 00:15:11.860
Does that mean if you pass 100 people, you
infect one of them?

